### 3.6 Background Study

The weak decay topology cuts used in the analysis reduce the background drastically since many particles produced at the primary vertex are cut away by applying the decay length and daughter track DCA cuts. However, backgrounds from other sources still need to be understood.

In d+Au collisions STAR TOF measurements [1] have shown that the proton(\(p\)) yield is about 10% of the \(\pi\) yield at low \(p_{T}\)(\(<1\) GeV/c) and the yield ratio of \(p/\pi\) increases with increasing \(p_{T}\) and is close to one at \(p_{T}~{}\simeq~{}3.0\) GeV/c [1]. For the low \(p_{T}\)\(K^{0}_{S}\), a large amount of the background comes from the combinatorial contribution of uncorrelated \(\pi^{+}\pi^{-}\) pairs since we use the \(dE/dx\) cuts(\(|n\sigma_{\pi}|<4\)) that ensures most of daughter tracks used are pions. Similarly for the low \(p_{T}\)\(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\), most of the background comes from the uncorrelated \(p\pi^{-}\) pairs.

